WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.660
Memory safety is central to RTOS security because many embedded systems run for long periods with limited memory and direct hardware interaction.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:15.960
Fixed buffers, stacks, heaps, memory pools, and shared objects all need clear limits and ownership.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.140
Predictable memory behavior is often more important than raw flexibility.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.620
Fixed buffers still require bounds checks.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:30.200
A buffer sized for a nominal message, sensor record, or diagnostic line may be too small for a boundary case.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.300
Code should validate lengths before copying, parsing, formatting, or appending.

7
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.440
The buffer size should be tied to the protocol or data contract, not only to an old example.

8
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.440
Stack usage needs special attention.

9
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:54.620
Each task has its own stack in many RTOS designs, and stack pressure may depend on call depth, local arrays, library calls, logging, and rare error paths.

10
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.920
Stack sizing should be supported by measurement, static review, and tests that exercise realistic worst cases.

11
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.720
Heap use should be governed by an explicit policy.

12
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.140
Some systems avoid dynamic allocation after startup.

13
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.880
Others use allocation in controlled layers.

14
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.740
If the heap is used, consider allocation failure, fragmentation, cleanup, and ownership.

15
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.240
The policy should be documented so reviewers know what is acceptable.

16
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.560
Memory pools can improve lifecycle control when they are sized and owned clearly.

17
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:34.900
A pool can bound allocation, reduce fragmentation, and make failure behavior predictable.

18
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:43.320
It still needs rules for who owns each object, when it is returned, what happens on exhaustion, and how stale references are prevented.

19
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:51.360
Where available, MPU-style isolation can reduce the impact of mistakes by limiting which tasks can access regions or peripherals.

20
00:01:51.460 --> 00:02:01.540
It is not a complete security solution by itself, but it can support a design that already defines task boundaries, stack regions, shared buffers, and privileged code paths.
